<h2>Authoring workflow</h2>
<p>Author the intact visual and fracture recipe in one scene or prefab:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Assembly root</strong> — an unparented entity with <code>Transform3D</code> and <code>Destructible3D</code>; an optional <code>Rigidbody3D</code> supplies body settings.</li>
  <li><strong>Participating visuals</strong> — child meshes with <code>Fracture3D</code>, or compact <code>Masonry3D</code> regions for repeated brickwork. The compiler prepares collider geometry and part mappings; do not add a second root collider or independent physics to those children.</li>
  <li><strong>Optional effects</strong> — <code>FractureDebris3D</code> on the root emits cosmetic chips on accepted spatial impacts. <code>debris_lifetime</code> on a fracture or masonry source controls fading of actual detached rubble.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Fracture3D</h3>
<p><code>Fracture3D</code> sits on a participating mesh and describes its generated pieces and bonds.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "Fracture3D": {
    "pieces": 12,
    "bond_health": 1.0,
    "anchor_below": 0.01
  }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>pieces</code> is the number of generated pieces; one piece can detach whole without further subdivision.</li>
  <li><code>bond_health</code> is authored connection strength. Spatial impact energy is converted to these units through the root's <code>energy_per_health</code>.</li>
  <li><code>anchor_below</code> attaches low parts to the foundation using an assembly-local Y threshold. Omit it when the object should start free.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Masonry3D</h3>
<p><code>Masonry3D</code> describes repeated brickwork: walls, stacks, and tiled panels that should crumble brick by brick.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "Masonry3D": {
    "rows": 6,
    "columns": 4,
    "size": [4, 3, 0.4],
    "density": 1800
  }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>rows</code> and <code>columns</code> define the compact brick grid over <code>size</code>.</li>
  <li><code>density</code> is mass density in kg/m³; it does not set bond health. Use <code>bond_health</code> for fracture resistance.</li>
  <li>Use masonry for repeated walls; use <code>Fracture3D</code> for authored meshes.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Destructible3D</h3>
<p><code>Destructible3D</code> marks the assembly root. It has no <code>integrity</code> field; connectivity comes from generated parts and their bonds.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "Destructible3D": {
    "energy_per_health": 1000,
    "max_bodies": 64
  }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>energy_per_health</code> converts spatial impact joules to authored bond-health units; it does not change <code>damage_part</code> units.</li>
  <li><code>max_bodies</code> bounds the physical body count for the assembly.</li>
  <li>Fracture and masonry sources supply the geometry. Foundation anchors determine whether a generated assembly starts static or dynamic.</li>
</ul>

<h3>FractureDebris3D</h3>
<p><code>FractureDebris3D</code> emits optional cosmetic chips at accepted spatial impact contacts. These chips are separate from detached physical rubble.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "FractureDebris3D": {
    "count": 16,
    "lifetime": 3.0,
    "fade_duration": 1.0
  }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>count</code> chips are emitted per accepted spatial impact, subject to <code>max_fragments</code>.</li>
  <li><code>lifetime</code> and <code>fade_duration</code> control chip removal. Chips have no entities, colliders, or physics bodies.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Applying damage at runtime</h2>
<p>Gameplay chooses when to issue a spatial impact or damage a known collider part. Both calls queue work for the next fixed step; acceptance is not completion.</p>
<pre><code>local Destruction = require("demi.physics.destruction3d")

local accepted, issue, affected = Destruction.impact({
    entity = "cargo_wall",             -- optional root/current-fragment filter
    position = { 0, 1, 0 },            -- required world-space point
    radius = 0.3,                      -- metres; default 0.5
    energy = 6000,                     -- joules shared across affected bonds
    impulse = 180,                     -- optional momentum budget in N*s
    direction = { 0, 0, 1 },           -- optional; omit for radial impulse
})
if not accepted then print(issue) end

local damaged, damage_issue = Destruction.damage_part("cargo_wall", "joint_l", 0.6)
if not damaged then print(damage_issue) end</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Arguments</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>impact(options)</code></td><td>Table with required <code>position</code> and at least one positive <code>energy</code> or <code>impulse</code>; optional <code>radius</code>, <code>direction</code>, <code>entity</code></td><td><code>accepted, error, affected_assemblies</code>. It selects nearby collider parts and shares the supplied budgets.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>damage_part(id, part_id, damage)</code></td><td>Root or current fragment ID, stable collider part ID, positive bond-health damage</td><td><code>accepted, error</code>. Queues damage to incident bonds and the part's foundation attachment.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>state(id)</code></td><td>Root or current fragment ID</td><td>A status table including revision and current part owners; see below.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><code>direction</code> is normalized internally; an omitted or zero direction makes impulses radial. Omit <code>entity</code> to affect all nearby attached destructible assemblies. Physical attachment requires the first physics step. Spatial hits do not automatically follow from ordinary collisions; gameplay must call <code>impact</code>.</p>
<p>A physics contact event can supply a point, normal, and measured impact energy. This script belongs on the wall and subscribes when it starts:</p>
<pre><code>local Events = require("demi.events")
local Destruction = require("demi.physics.destruction3d")
local Wall = {}

function Wall:on_start()
    self.contact_token = Events.subscribe("physics3d_contact", function(e)
        if e.entity_id ~= self.entity_id or e.is_trigger or e.impact_energy &lt;= 0 then
            return
        end
        local accepted, issue = Destruction.impact({
            entity = self.entity_id,
            position = { e.point_x, e.point_y, e.point_z },
            direction = { e.normal_x, e.normal_y, e.normal_z },
            energy = e.impact_energy,
            radius = 0.3,
        })
        if not accepted then print(issue) end
    end)
end

function Wall:on_destroy()
    Events.unsubscribe(self.contact_token)
end

return Wall</code></pre>
<p>The contact event exposes <code>entity_id</code>, <code>other_entity_id</code>, flattened point and normal coordinates, and <code>impact_energy</code>. The example filters to this wall and skips zero-energy contacts. <code>energy_per_health</code> on the assembly converts impact joules into bond damage; an accepted call can still leave a structure intact.</p>
<p>Targeting one joint when a boss exposes a weak point:</p>
<pre><code>local Destruction = require("demi.physics.destruction3d")

local accepted, issue = Destruction.damage_part(id, "joint_l", 0.6)
if not accepted then print(issue) end</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>id</code> is the assembly root or one of its current fragment entities.</li>
  <li><code>"joint_l"</code> is a stable collider part ID, not a generated array index.</li>
  <li><code>0.6</code> is positive damage in authored bond-health units. Check <code>state</code> after the fixed step for the committed result.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Reading state</h2>
<pre><code>local Destruction = require("demi.physics.destruction3d")
local state = Destruction.state("cargo_wall")
-- state.root, state.status, state.error, state.revision, state.bodies
-- state.parts[part_id] is the part's current physical entity ID</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>root</code></td><td>Owning assembly root ID. An empty value with <code>unattached</code> means no attached assembly was found.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td><code>unattached</code>, <code>ready</code>, <code>queued</code>, <code>applied</code>, or <code>failed</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>error</code></td><td>Failure detail when a queued transaction fails.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>revision</code></td><td>Advances after successful committed batches; compare it with the value before queuing damage.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>bodies</code></td><td>Current number of physical bodies in the assembly.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>parts</code></td><td>Map from stable collider part IDs to their current physical entity IDs.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><code>state</code> returns a table, including for an unattached ID. Check <code>status</code> and <code>error</code>, then use <code>revision</code>, <code>bodies</code>, or <code>parts</code> for gameplay. It does not expose a single integrity meter, a <code>broken</code> flag, or a fragment-ID array. Resolve a part's current physical owner through <code>parts</code> rather than constructing generated IDs.</p>

<h2>Checkpoint and restore</h2>
<p><code>checkpoint</code> returns a versioned, geometry-free snapshot of committed damage and fragment poses. Save it if the game needs later recovery. <code>restore</code> validates that snapshot against a <strong>fresh attached assembly</strong> with matching geometry and part identities, then queues restoration. It does not rewind the already damaged live assembly in place.</p>
<pre><code>local Destruction = require("demi.physics.destruction3d")

local snapshot, issue = Destruction.checkpoint("cargo_wall")
if not snapshot then error(issue) end
-- Persist snapshot through demi.save if it must survive a restart.

-- Later, after loading a fresh matching cargo_wall assembly:
local accepted, restore_issue = Destruction.restore("cargo_wall", snapshot)
if not accepted then error(restore_issue) end
-- Poll Destruction.state("cargo_wall") after the fixed step.</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Arguments</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>checkpoint(id)</code></td><td>Assembly root or current fragment ID</td><td>Snapshot table and error string, or <code>nil, error</code> while commands are queued or the assembly is unavailable.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>restore(id, snapshot)</code></td><td>Fresh attached assembly and a prior checkpoint table</td><td><code>accepted, error</code>. Poll <code>state</code> for completion or failure.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Only committed damage can be captured. A matching fresh assembly must finish its first physics attachment before restoration, and incompatible geometry or part IDs are rejected. A retry that simply needs the authored pristine scene can reload that scene; a checkpoint is for restoring a specific prior damage state.</p>

<h2>Debris cleanup</h2>
<p>Cosmetic chips from <code>FractureDebris3D</code> expire after their authored lifetime. Separately, <code>retire_debris</code> queues opt-in cleanup of detached dynamic physical groups while preserving supported geometry.</p>
<pre><code>local Destruction = require("demi.physics.destruction3d")

local accepted, issue = Destruction.retire_debris("cargo_wall")
if not accepted then print(issue) end</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>id</code> identifies the owning assembly.</li>
  <li>The call returns <code>accepted, error</code>; the fixed step performs the retirement.</li>
  <li>Retired detached groups are recorded as missing in checkpoints. Use this deliberately if those physical pieces matter to gameplay.</li>
</ul>
<p>For fading actual detached rubble without a Lua cleanup call, set positive <code>debris_lifetime</code> and <code>debris_fade</code> on its <code>Fracture3D</code> or <code>Masonry3D</code> source. Those chunks become non-colliding while fading; connected supported chunks stay physical.</p>

<h2>Putting it together</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Author a normal scene or prefab with <code>Destructible3D</code> at the assembly root and <code>Fracture3D</code> meshes or <code>Masonry3D</code> regions beneath it.</li>
  <li>Call <code>impact(options)</code> with world-space position and positive energy or impulse, or <code>damage_part</code> with a stable collider part ID.</li>
  <li>Read <code>state.status</code>, <code>revision</code>, <code>bodies</code>, and <code>parts</code> to observe committed changes.</li>
  <li>Capture a checkpoint after queued work commits; restore it only onto a fresh matching assembly.</li>
  <li>Use source <code>debris_lifetime</code> for fading detached rubble; <code>FractureDebris3D</code> supplies separate cosmetic chips.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cosmetic dents belong to mesh deformation, while physical pieces use ordinary 3D body behavior described in <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'rigidbodies'}) }}">Rigidbodies</a>.</p>
